 <section class="changed">
 <h2>What changed</h2>
 <p>The Cyber Resilience Act applies broadly to products with digital elements made available on the EU market when their intended or reasonably foreseeable use includes a direct or indirect logical or physical data connection. It covers design, development and production requirements, vulnerability-handling processes and market surveillance; machinery can be subject both to the CRA and the Machinery Regulation. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-scope --></p>
-<p>The first operational deadline is immediate. From 11 September 2026 manufacturers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe security incidents. An early warning is due within 24 hours, a fuller notification within 72 hours, and the vulnerability final report no later than fourteen days after a corrective or mitigating measure is available. The main CRA obligations apply from 11 December 2027. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-dates --> ENISA says its Single Reporting Platform is the tool manufacturers and CSIRTs will use from that first date. <!-- CHECK: 01-srp --></p>
+<p>CRA reporting starts on 11 September 2026. From that date, manufacturers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe security incidents. An early warning is due within 24 hours and a fuller notification within 72 hours. The vulnerability final report follows no later than fourteen days after a corrective or mitigating measure becomes available. The main CRA obligations apply from 11 December 2027. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-dates --> ENISA says its Single Reporting Platform is the tool manufacturers and CSIRTs will use from 11 September 2026. <!-- CHECK: 01-srp --></p>
 <p>The longer-lived workload matters more than the filing. A manufacturer must document relevant cybersecurity aspects and vulnerabilities, keep the risk assessment current where appropriate, operate coordinated vulnerability disclosure, and handle vulnerabilities through a support period normally at least five years. The Act specifically notes that industrial products are often used much longer; technical documentation must be continuously updated where appropriate during the support period. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-lifecycle --> This is the recurring state: product releases alter components; suppliers disclose faults; new vulnerabilities change exploitability; field configurations change exposure; a fix changes evidence; a customer report changes the incident record; support clocks expire.</p>
-<p>The Data Act creates a different queue. Its connected-product definition covers an item that obtains, generates or collects use or environmental data and can communicate product data, where storing or transmitting data for someone other than the user is not the primary function. The Commission explicitly gives robots and industrial machinery as examples. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-scope --> Article 3 requires connected products and related services to make product data, related-service data and necessary metadata accessible by default, easily, securely, free of charge and in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format; pre-contract information must describe the data and access route. Where data are not directly accessible, Article 4 requires the data holder to provide readily available data on a simple electronic request where feasible. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --></p>
-<p>The Data Act has applied generally since 12 September 2025, but the Article 3(1) design obligation applies to connected products and related services placed on the market after 12 September 2026. Trade secrets do not create a casual blanket exemption: the Act sets out confidentiality measures, written substantiation and notifications to the competent authority when data are withheld or suspended. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-dates --> <!-- CHECK: 01-data-secrets --> That produces more state changes: a new sensor or firmware build alters the data catalogue; a contract changes permitted use; a user or third party requests access; security or trade-secret controls change the release decision; an interface or retention rule changes.</p>
+<p>The Data Act creates a different queue. Its connected-product definition covers an item that obtains, generates or collects use or environmental data and can communicate product data. Storing or transmitting data for someone other than the user cannot be its primary function. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-scope --> The Commission gives robots and industrial machines as examples. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-examples --> Article 3 requires accessible product data, related-service data and necessary metadata by default. Access must be easy, secure, free and in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format. Pre-contract information must describe the data and access route. Where data are not directly accessible, Article 4 requires the data holder to provide readily available data on a simple electronic request where feasible. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --></p>
+<p>The Data Act has applied generally since 12 September 2025. Its Article 3(1) design obligation covers connected products and related services placed on the market after 12 September 2026. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-dates --> Trade secrets do not create a casual blanket exemption. The Act sets out confidentiality measures, written substantiation and notifications to the competent authority when data are withheld or suspended. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-secrets --> That produces more state changes. A new sensor or firmware build alters the data catalogue. A contract changes permitted use, while a user or third party may request access. Security or trade-secret controls change the release decision. An interface or retention rule can also change.</p>
 <p>These laws overlap around product identity, versions, ownership and change control, but they do not become one discipline. CRA interpretation belongs with product-security and conformity specialists. Data Act interpretation belongs with counsel and commercial/product owners. Technical implementation can share an evidence graph and release gates. Selling “combined compliance” without that separation invites bad legal advice and confuses two budgets.</p>
 <p>Regulation proves workload, not paid demand. The best directly relevant field evidence found here is a 2024 qualitative survey of twelve Northern European organisations: seven large industrial-equipment manufacturers and five consultancies or partners, recruited partly through a compliance-automation research project. All surveyed manufacturers already had an IEC 62443-4-1-aligned secure-development lifecycle, so they are not the proposed small customer. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-sample --> Respondents described ownership, secure-development enforcement, update management, expertise and 24-hour notification as difficult; they found organisational and procedural issues harder than many technical controls. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-pain --> Yet half expected CRA cost increases below 20%, a quarter expected no cost impact, and half expected employee roles to remain unchanged. The authors explicitly suggest firms may use existing resources. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-budget --> That is evidence of pain and a warning against assuming a budget. No source here shows a small industrial OEM paying an external operator each month.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="sale">
 <h2>The first sale</h2>
-<p>Build a list of ten prospect types, not a mailing list of everyone with an Ethernet port: packaging-line OEM; food-processing machine builder; industrial-weighing maker; warehouse-equipment OEM; water-treatment skid maker; specialist robot cell producer; machine-vision equipment maker; connected pump or compressor maker; retrofit gateway producer; and private-label industrial controller vendor. Each must ship its own branded product, control at least part of the software release, and lack a visible product-security operations team. Importers and distributors are a later segment because their duties and leverage differ.</p>
-<p>Reach them through three routes: English-language product-security roundtables with FME/Agoria-type industry groups; introductions from CE/machinery-safety advisers and embedded-development firms; and direct, product-specific notes to engineering or quality leaders. The note names one observable trigger—an approaching release, a public vulnerability contact, a customer data promise—not “the CRA market.” Dutch A2 is insufficient for trust-heavy cold selling into small local factories. The operating hypothesis therefore requires English-capable export manufacturers and a bilingual partner for Dutch workshops and manuals. If the first ten insist that commercial and technical work happen in Dutch, this route is not founder-led.</p>
-<p>The sale is a €7,500 baseline, paid, for one family and capped at four weeks. It includes one incident tabletop and one real data-export/request test; it does not promise conformity. At closeout, the buyer receives the artefacts even if it declines the retainer. The continuation offer is €3,000 per month for weekly triage, monthly evidence review and quarterly drills, with implementation projects separately scoped. These are required-price hypotheses, not observed market prices.</p>
-<p>The signer should be the person who can assign engineering and quality time. A security enthusiast without that authority cannot buy an operating function because every closed item needs internal judgement or code. “Why now” is the reporting process due in days and the need to learn it before an actual incident, but urgency earns a meeting, not a purchase order. The diagnostic is designed to reveal whether the buyer values closure or merely wants a gap report.</p>
+<p>Build ten prospect types inside food and packaging machinery, not a mailing list of every connected-device maker. Include packaging-line, tray-sealing, filling, dosing, slicing, weighing, inspection, bakery-process, conveyor and palletising-cell OEMs. Each must ship its own branded product, control part of the software release, and lack a visible product-security operations team. Warehouse equipment, water-treatment skids and general industrial controllers are later expansion options. Importers and distributors also come later because their duties and leverage differ.</p>
+<p>Reach them through English-language product-security roundtables, adviser introductions and direct product-specific notes to engineering leaders. The note names one observable trigger, such as an approaching release or public vulnerability contact. It does not pitch “the CRA market.” Dutch A2 is insufficient for trust-heavy cold selling into small local factories. The direct hypothesis therefore targets English-capable export manufacturers. A bilingual facilitator is optional for a customer-requested Dutch workshop or manual. That work is separately quoted, customer-funded and carries no referral share; the operation keeps the customer contract. If all ten prospects require Dutch commercial and technical delivery, stop the direct route.</p>
+<p>The CRA sale is a paid €7,500 baseline for one family, capped at four weeks. It includes one incident tabletop and does not promise conformity. At closeout, the buyer receives the artefacts even if it declines the retainer. The continuation offer is €3,000 per month for weekly triage, monthly evidence review and quarterly drills. Implementation projects are separately scoped.</p>
+<p>The product director may separately buy a €2,500 Data Act module. It maps generated and retrievable data, records counsel-approved release rules and tests one real request. Counsel contracts directly with the manufacturer. The recurring model excludes this module until payment proves recurring demand. All figures are required-price design hurdles, not observed market prices.</p>
+<p>The CRA signer is the engineering director who can assign engineering and quality time. A security enthusiast without that authority cannot buy an operating function because each closed item needs internal judgement or code. The dated trigger is the 11 September 2026 reporting start and the need to rehearse before an incident. Urgency earns a meeting, not a purchase order. The baseline reveals whether the buyer values closure or merely wants a gap report.</p>
