-<p>Year one fails the owner-income gate and should: three pilot clients cannot pay a senior founder and build the method. The mature case passes only if three delivery engineers can support twenty customers while the founder moves to sales, escalation and product. That is roughly seven customers per delivery engineer, a capacity assumption to test with time logs. If each customer consumes a day a week, the model collapses into a consultancy.</p>
+<p>Year one fails the owner-income gate and should. Three pilot clients cannot pay a senior founder and build the method. Its €27,000 delivery hurdle is transparent: €12,000 of contract support, €9,000 of tooling and €6,000 of secure cloud and backup.</p>
+<p>The mature case uses one analyst and two engineers while the founder moves to sales, escalation and product. At 120 productive hours per hire each month, the team has 360 hours. Retainers consume a designed 240 hours monthly: 20 clients at 12 hours each. Ten annual diagnostics average another 50 hours monthly. That is 81% planned utilization, leaving 70 hours for variance and internal improvement. If a client regularly consumes a day each week, the model collapses into a consultancy.</p>
 <p>Recurring revenue is €720,000 in the mature model, 86% of revenue. It is earned by changing customer state, not an invented annual report. Gross margin pays delivery staff and software before corporate overhead. The €100,000 founder line is owner compensation before personal tax at replacement cost; the €310,000 surplus is not salary and should mostly remain available for hiring, tax and product investment.</p>
-<p>Working capital is the constraint hidden by annual arithmetic. A three-person delivery payroll plus software and tax can consume about €90,000 before receivables catch up in this model. Do not hire that team against unsigned pipeline. Bill diagnostics 50% in advance, retainers monthly in advance, keep customer-paid laboratory work out of the books, and build the payroll reserve from retained pilot cash. The initial €100,000 is enough only if growth follows receipts.</p>
+<p>Working capital is the constraint hidden by annual arithmetic. Hire the analyst only after five signed retainers, the first engineer after ten, and the second after fifteen. Bill diagnostics 50% in advance and retainers monthly in advance. The €90,000 reserve hurdle comprises three months of mature delivery cost, or €75,000, plus about six weeks of annual overhead, rounded to €15,000. It covers hiring before capacity is fully used and one late collection cycle. Keep customer-paid laboratory work outside the books. The initial €100,000 is enough only if hiring follows receipts.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="scale">
 <h2>From service to company</h2>
-<p><strong>Customer one</strong> is founder work. The asset is not software; it is the first complete state model: product, version, component, vulnerability, decision, evidence, data field, request, owner and deadline. Every action records input, decision authority, output and elapsed time. The founder learns where legal judgement ends and implementation begins.</p>
-<p><strong>Customer five</strong> uses one canonical playbook with product-specific adapters. A trained analyst runs intake, evidence checks and meeting preparation; an engineer handles pipelines and export tests; the founder handles risk escalation. At least 60% of routine hours must be performed without the founder, and every customer must use the same weekly queue and severity rules. Acquisition begins to repeat through two adviser or embedded-firm channels, not five founder friendships.</p>
+<p><strong>Customer one</strong> is founder work. The asset is not software; it is the first CRA state model: product, version, component, vulnerability, decision, evidence, owner and deadline. A purchased Data Act module adds data fields and requests to those shared identities. Every action records input, decision authority, output and elapsed time. The founder learns where legal judgement ends and implementation begins.</p>
+<p><strong>Customer five</strong> uses one canonical playbook with product-specific adapters. A trained analyst runs intake, evidence checks and meeting preparation. An engineer handles pipelines and tests; the founder handles risk escalation. At least 60% of routine hours must occur without the founder. Every customer uses the same weekly queue and severity rules. Acquisition begins to repeat through adviser introductions and direct referrals, not five founder friendships.</p>
 <p><strong>Customer twenty</strong> has a multi-tenant workflow product only where reuse is real: immutable decision history, release evidence, supplier/component links, deadline timers, role-based access and exportable technical-file indexes. Customer code, risk acceptance and legal opinions stay outside or behind strict tenant boundaries. Integrations into Git, issue tracking and document stores are the accumulating asset; so are elapsed-time benchmarks and playbooks. The system does not decide whether a product is legally in scope or whether disclosure would cause serious economic damage.</p>
 <p>The four scalability tests are therefore explicit. Acquisition repeats only if two channels each create several paid baselines a year. Delivery is teachable only if analysts close most queue items from written procedures. Value recurs only while releases, vulnerabilities, requests and support states change. The asset accumulates only if integrations and evidence history reduce the next customer's labour. Fail any two and the honest outcome is a good expert practice, not a scalable company.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="competition">
 <h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
-<p>ONEKEY already markets firmware/SBOM analysis, vulnerability monitoring, impact assessment and a compliance workflow for manufacturers, combined with consulting. Cybellum offers a product-security platform spanning SBOMs, risk, vulnerabilities, incident response and regulatory evidence. <!-- CHECK: 01-onekey --> <!-- CHECK: 01-cybellum --> The EU-funded CRACoWi project states a value of €8.44 million and a term through August 2027; its wizard is intended to help SMEs, manufacturers, importers and distributors automate CRA assessments and documentation. <!-- CHECK: 01-cracowi --> These are not distant category competitors. They can add service, partner with assessors, lower prices or become the customer's system of record.</p>
+<p>ONEKEY already markets firmware/SBOM analysis, vulnerability monitoring, impact assessment and a compliance workflow for manufacturers, combined with consulting. <!-- CHECK: 01-onekey --> Cybellum offers a product-security platform spanning SBOMs, risk, vulnerabilities, incident response and regulatory evidence. <!-- CHECK: 01-cybellum --> The EU-funded CRACoWi project states a value of €8.44 million and a term through August 2027. Its wizard is intended to help SMEs, manufacturers, importers and distributors automate CRA assessments and documentation. <!-- CHECK: 01-cracowi --> These are not distant category competitors. They can add service, partner with assessors, lower prices or become the customer's system of record.</p>
 <p>Generic substitutes are stronger than their product pages suggest. A competent quality manager can join an SBOM scanner, Jira, SharePoint, counsel and an annual penetration test. An embedded supplier can add the work to an existing development retainer. A CE adviser can own the evidence map. The manufacturer may reasonably prefer internal risk acceptance and customer communication because outsourcing adds another sensitive repository and another party during a 24-hour incident.</p>
 <p>Standards will also move the boundary. CEN and CENELEC's 2026 programme says horizontal CRA drafts, vulnerability-handling work and industrial vertical profiles are still being advanced, with key work scheduled for the end of 2026. <!-- CHECK: 01-standards --> Clearer standards help delivery but commoditise the map. The operation must own reliable closure across tools, not proprietary interpretations that disappear when guidance improves.</p>
 <p>The response is deliberately narrow: serve long-lived industrial products where firmware extraction, field variants, intermittent connectivity, supplier images and safe update windows make an ordinary SaaS workflow incomplete. Integrate ONEKEY, Cybellum or open tools when they fit. Never build an SBOM scanner merely to avoid paying a vendor. The defensible margin, if any, sits in cross-functional operation and accumulated customer context.</p>
+<p>The Data Act work remains a separately priced implementation module, not a combined recurring product. Its substitutes are counsel, internal product owners, contract repositories and ordinary API integrators. A bilingual facilitator can also become the prime contractor and squeeze the margin. Direct English contracting and customer-funded language support are therefore part of the test, not details to settle after sale.</p>
 </section>
 
