 <p class="kicker">Essay 1 of 7 · Connected Product Compliance Operations</p>
 <h1>The law creates a queue, not a customer</h1>
-<p class="lede">A Dutch machine builder is entering a period in which two obligations attach to one connected product: keep its software secure through a long operating life, and make defined product data accessible on defined terms. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-lifecycle --> <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --> The attractive business is not another readiness report. It is the small operating team that keeps product, component, vulnerability, evidence and data-access records true after the report is filed. The fatal possibility is simpler: manufacturers may buy a lawyer, an assessor and a software licence once, then give the recurring queue to existing quality and engineering staff. The first investment decision is therefore not whether the regulation is important. It is whether one narrow manufacturer will pay an outsider to own the queue.</p>
+<p class="lede">A Dutch machine builder is entering a period in which two obligations attach to one connected product: secure its software through a long operating life, and make defined product data accessible on defined terms. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-lifecycle --> <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --> The attractive business is not another readiness report. It is a small team that keeps the product-security record true after filing. Data-access work shares inputs but has a different buyer. The fatal possibility is simpler: manufacturers may buy advice and software once, then give both queues to existing staff. The investment decision is whether one narrow manufacturer will pay an outsider to operate the CRA queue and buy Data Act implementation separately.</p>
 
 <section class="proposition">
 <h2>The proposition</h2>
-<p>The first customer is a Netherlands- or Belgium-based maker of connected food-processing or packaging machinery with its own firmware or control software, no dedicated product-security operations team, and several product variants still supported in the field. The economic buyer is the engineering director or COO; quality is the internal sponsor. The purchase event is a dated product release, a customer security questionnaire, or a management review that discovers nobody can demonstrate who owns vulnerability intake and data-access requests.</p>
-<p>The initial deliverable is a four-week, fixed-scope <em>product operations baseline</em> for one product family. It produces a product-and-version register, an SBOM intake path, a vulnerability triage queue, an incident-notification rehearsal, a support-period record, an evidence index, a map of generated and retrievable product data, and a tested user-request path. The continuation is a managed weekly queue with a named customer owner: changes enter, decisions are assigned, evidence is linked, deadlines are rehearsed, and stale items are escalated.</p>
+<p>The first customer is a Netherlands- or Belgium-based maker of connected food-processing or packaging machinery. It owns firmware or control software, lacks a dedicated product-security operations team, and supports several product variants in the field. The CRA economic buyer is the engineering director. This person can assign engineering and quality work and owns the release decision. The purchase event is a dated release, a customer security questionnaire, or a management review that finds no owner for vulnerability intake.</p>
